# Cells whose difference is not the text stack, on Firefox.
#
# Companion to widget-chrome.accept, which covers the two engines' shared
# `forms/` cells. Everything here sits outside that directory and is Gecko's
# own, so it is kept separate rather than widening that file's scope. Three
# classes, each attributed rather than assumed.
#
# The textarea resize grabber. Every one of these charges its whole difference
# to a <TEXTAREA> and every one differs by exactly 149 px, whatever the page
# puts in the control: 24px Hebrew, a 34px webfont, and - the one that settles
# it - placeholder-sideways-crash, whose textarea is empty and still differs by
# the same 149 px. A 149 px cluster in the corner of an empty control is the
# resizer, which GTK draws here and the Windows theme draws there.
wt/external/wpt/css/css-writing-modes/block-plaintext-005.html
wt/external/wpt/css/css-writing-modes/placeholder-sideways-crash.html
wt/external/wpt/css/css-writing-modes/writing-mode-vrl-forms-00[34]-manual.html
# The same grabber in css-fonts. Both pages are a bare <textarea>, the text in
# it matches, and the whole 149 px sits in one 16x16 corner. A shimmed and an
# unshimmed Linux draw that corner byte for byte alike, 98 px of ink against
# the 145 px Windows draws, so nothing the shim answers reaches it.
wt/external/wpt/css/css-fonts/font-size-monospace-adjust.html
wt/external/wpt/css/css-fonts/font-size-monospace-adjust-ref.html
# The same grabber again, in css-inline, css-lists and css-pseudo. Every
# differing pixel on these pages is charged to a <TEXTAREA> and every control
# contributes one 16x16 corner of 149 px, whatever font or text it holds; a
# page with six controls differs by six of them. One reads 155 px at a max
# channel difference of 83 where the corner overlaps the control below it.
wt/external/wpt/css/css-inline/baseline-source/baseline-source-first-textarea-00[123].tentative.html
wt/external/wpt/css/css-inline/baseline-source/baseline-source-last-textarea-00[123].tentative.html
